<h2>2. Problem Statement &amp; Clinical Motivation</h2>
<p>Cervical cancer is one of the most preventable and treatable cancers when detected early. The gold standard for early detection is <strong>Papanicolaou (Pap) smear testing</strong>: a pathologist examines cells scraped from the cervix under a microscope and classifies them. This process is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Slow</strong> — a single slide can contain thousands of cells, each needing manual inspection</li>
<li><strong>Subjective</strong> — results vary between clinicians, especially for borderline cases</li>
<li><strong>Resource-intensive</strong> — requires trained cytotechnologists, which are scarce in low-income regions</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The AI solution:</strong> An automated pipeline that receives a microscope slide image, finds every individual cell automatically, measures its physical morphology, analyzes its visual texture, and returns a clinical diagnosis in real time.</p>
<h3>The 5 Clinical Classes</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Class ID</th>
<th>Name</th>
<th>Clinical Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td><strong>Dyskeratotic</strong></td>
<td>⚠️ Abnormal cells with hardened keratin — indicator of dysplasia and potential malignancy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td><strong>Koilocytotic</strong></td>
<td>⚠️ Cells with a nuclear "halo" — pathognomonic sign of HPV infection</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td><strong>Metaplastic</strong></td>
<td>✅ Cells that changed shape due to irritation — generally benign</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td><strong>Parabasal</strong></td>
<td>⚠️ Small, round, immature cells from deeper tissue — can indicate abnormality</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td><strong>Superficial-Intermediate</strong></td>
<td>✅ Healthy mature cells from the surface epithelium — normal finding</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Critical Classes (flagged red in UI):</strong> Dyskeratotic, Koilocytotic, Parabasal</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
