<h2>7. Feature Engineering Deep Dive</h2>
<p>The 51 features span 8 biological categories extracted entirely from U-Net masks, OpenCV mathematics, and EfficientNet probabilities.</p>
<h3>Category 1: Core Morphometry (4 features)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Formula</th>
<th>Medical Significance</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Area</code></td>
<td><code>sum(mask == 2)</code></td>
<td>Enlarged nucleus = suspicious</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Cyt_Area</code></td>
<td><code>sum(mask == 1)</code></td>
<td>Cytoplasm area</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Total_Area</code></td>
<td><code>Nuc + Cyt</code></td>
<td>Overall cell size</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>NC_Ratio</code></td>
<td><code>Nuc_Area / Cyt_Area</code></td>
<td><strong>#1 clinical biomarker</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Category 2: Shape Descriptors (7 features)</h3>
<p>From <code>cv2.findContours</code> on the nucleus mask:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Source</th>
<th>Significance</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Perimeter</code></td>
<td><code>cv2.arcLength</code></td>
<td>Jagged edge = irregular nucleus</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Circularity</code></td>
<td><code>4π·A/P²</code></td>
<td>Cancer cells less circular</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Eccentricity</code></td>
<td><code>cv2.fitEllipse</code></td>
<td>Elongated nucleus = dysplastic</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nucleus_Aspect</code></td>
<td>Ellipse axes</td>
<td>Asymmetry measure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nucleus_Solidity</code></td>
<td><code>A/ConvexHullA</code></td>
<td>Concavities in boundary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nucleus_Extent</code></td>
<td><code>A/BoundingRect</code></td>
<td>Fill density</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Centroid_Dist</code></td>
<td>Nuc vs Cyt centroid</td>
<td>Nuclear eccentricity</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Category 3: Hu Moments (7 features)</h3>
<p><code>Hu_1</code> – <code>Hu_7</code> — Rotation-invariant shape fingerprint from <code>cv2.HuMoments</code>.</p>
<h3>Category 4: Fourier Shape Descriptors (5 features)</h3>
<p><code>Fourier_1</code> – <code>Fourier_5</code> — FFT magnitudes of the nucleus boundary contour. Low frequencies = gross shape; high frequencies = edge roughness.</p>
<h3>Category 5: Chromatin Intensity Statistics (7 features)</h3>
<p>Computed on grayscale pixels <em>inside</em> the nucleus mask only:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Significance</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>Chromatin_Variance</code></td>
<td>Rough texture = dysplasia hallmark</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Mean</code></td>
<td>Average brightness</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Median</code></td>
<td>Robust central brightness</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Std</code></td>
<td>Pixel variability</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Skew</code></td>
<td>Asymmetric brightness = hyperchromasia</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nuc_Kurtosis</code></td>
<td>Peakedness of distribution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Nucleus_Entropy</code></td>
<td>Shannon entropy of histogram</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Category 6: GLCM Texture Features (4 features)</h3>
<p>Gray Level Co-occurrence Matrix on 64×64 downscaled nucleus, averaged over 4 angles (0°, 45°, 90°, 135°):</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Significance</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>GLCM_Contrast</code></td>
<td>Local intensity variation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>GLCM_Correlation</code></td>
<td>Pixel relationship patterns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>GLCM_Energy</code></td>
<td>Textural uniformity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>GLCM_Homogeneity</code></td>
<td>Distribution diagonality</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Category 7: LBP Histogram (9 features)</h3>
<p><code>LBP_0</code> – <code>LBP_8</code> — 9-bin uniform Local Binary Pattern histogram (<code>P=8, R=1</code>). Rotation-invariant fine texture descriptor. Highly effective for chromatin granularity discrimination.</p>
<h3>Category 8: Color Ratios + CNN Probabilities (8 features)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Source</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>RG_Ratio</code>, <code>RB_Ratio</code>, <code>GB_Ratio</code></td>
<td>Nuclear staining intensity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Prob_0</code> – <code>Prob_4</code></td>
<td>EfficientNet softmax output</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>8. Super Ensemble Architecture</h2>
<h3>Motivation</h3>
<p>Each model has unique inductive biases:
- <strong>XGBoost</strong> — fast, handles sparse features well
- <strong>LightGBM</strong> — leaf-wise growth, typically better generalization
- <strong>Random Forest</strong> — bagging provides natural variance reduction</p>
<p>Hard Majority Voting requires 2-of-3 agreement, eliminating single-model failure modes.</p>
<h3>Implementation</h3>
<pre><code class="language-python">class CervicalCancerSuperEnsemble:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Hard Majority Vote across XGBoost, LightGBM, Random Forest.&quot;&quot;&quot;

    def predict(self, X):
        p1 = self.xgb.predict(X[self.xgb_feats])   # 13 features
        p2 = self.lgb.predict(X[self.lgb_feats])   # 20 features
        p3 = self.rf.predict(X[self.rf_feats])     # 9 features
        stacked = np.stack([p1, p2, p3], axis=1)
        return scipy.stats.mode(stacked, axis=1).mode

    def predict_proba(self, X):
        # Soft average for confidence scores shown in UI
        return (self.xgb.predict_proba(X[self.xgb_feats]) +
                self.lgb.predict_proba(X[self.lgb_feats]) +
                self.rf.predict_proba(X[self.rf_feats])) / 3.0
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Each sub-model was feature-selected independently (XGBoost: 13 features, LightGBM: 20, Random Forest: 9). The StandardScaler is fit once on all 51 features.</p>
</blockquote>
