<h2>12. Key Design Decisions &amp; Lessons Learned</h2>
<h3>1. Why Not Just Use EfficientNet Directly?</h3>
<p>EfficientNet alone achieves ~97.41% accuracy but is fragile to microscope defocus, staining variability, and imaging conditions. Morphological features (NC Ratio, shape descriptors) are invariant to visual quality — they measure physical structure, not appearance. The Super Ensemble remains robust even with degraded inputs.</p>
<h3>2. Why 51 Features Instead of 13?</h3>
<p>The original 13-feature pipeline (8 morphological + 5 CNN probabilities) was good. The expanded 51-feature set adds GLCM texture, LBP histogram, Hu Moments, Fourier descriptors, and color channel ratios — particularly helping discriminate Koilocytotic (HPV) from Metaplastic cells, which are visually similar but clinically very different.</p>
<h3>3. CUDA OOM Management</h3>
<p>Running 5 DL models simultaneously on a 6GB GPU is infeasible. U-Net and EfficientNet run on CPU for single-cell inference. XGBoost was forced to CPU via <code>set_param({"device": "cpu"})</code> to prevent a fatal C++ crash unique to Windows GPU/CPU device mismatch.</p>
<h3>4. Hard Majority Voting vs Stacking</h3>
<p>Stacking (training a meta-model on predictions) risks data leakage and overfitting with 4,049 samples. Hard majority voting is parameter-free, zero-overfitting-risk, and the 2-of-3 threshold provides natural confidence qualification.</p>
<h3>5. GLCM Memory Safety</h3>
<p>Full 128×128 GLCM creates 256×256×1×4 matrices per cell. Across 4,049 cells this causes memory fragmentation. Downscaling to 64×64 before GLCM reduces memory 4× while preserving discriminative texture information.</p>
<hr />
